Z||000003||Tigger||11-30-2000||04:28 PM||||I have an '84 SE also.<P>Yep, last time I checked that's where they are.<P>Carefully pry the speaker covers on the dash, and they will come off, ones in the headrest are difficult to get too.  You would have to remove the seat cover, which is a pain.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Sndfr||11-30-2000||05:33 PM||||I thought so too but no sound comes out of the headrests and i cant <I>feel</I> anything in there either.<P>------------------<BR>-<B>matthew d.</B><BR><I>"who's yo daddy?"</I><P>'84 SE||||reg||9
Z||000005||Monkeyman||11-30-2000||06:33 PM||||First, make sure that the speakers are plugged in.  There is a connector by the inside rear of each seat.  Also make sure that someone didn't swap seats with a post-'85 model.  Your seats should have a bunch of holes in the front of the headrests.  It would seem unlikely that all 4 speakers (2 in each headrest) would be bad.  I have an '85 with over 110k miles on it and the seat speakers still work.||||reg||1
Z||000006||KRMFiero||12-01-2000||09:31 PM||||If you want to replace the speakers in the headrest... the only way i can discribe it is EASY... I did it in less the 1 hour and im 16 and well not the smartest  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">....   just unzip, have plyers handy, undo the rod thingy.... pull up- pull it as far as you can up then you have to just pull very Strongy on like the back part and you can just leave on the from wire twisty things on the head rest (pull the cover over the headrest) then just pull the speakers right out and put new ones in...., reverse the proccess.     I replaced mine with AudioBahn 3.5's and i think they sound really good, I was extreamly surprised at how easy this is to do but i reccomend you take the seat out to do this, four bolts<P>Kyle||||reg||1
